1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,360
Out here, fog does more than hide the road.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,580
It changes what a driver can trust.

3
00:00:05,380 --> 00:00:09,080
Is that shape a vehicle,
a person, or the edge of the road?

4
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:11,520
We started with that uncertainty.

5
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,380
Our challenge wasn't simply to see farther.

6
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:17,680
It was to know when the evidence
was no longer good enough.

7
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:20,140
So we built TARK around a question:

8
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:24,060
when visibility drops,
how do we make a more informed decision?

9
00:00:24,780 --> 00:00:25,780
Let us show you.

10
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:29,200
This engineering demonstrator
brings sensing interfaces,

11
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:32,960
reasoning, bounded control,
and monitoring together.

12
00:00:34,020 --> 00:00:37,660
You're seeing conceptual hardware alongside
our integrated software demonstration,

13
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:40,460
not a claim of completed mine testing.

14
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:43,200
Different sensors answer
different questions.

15
00:00:43,740 --> 00:00:45,460
Radar provides local targets.

16
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,840
Cameras add visual context;

17
00:00:48,760 --> 00:00:50,340
thermal sensing adds temperature patterns.

18
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:53,040
The inertial sensor describes orientation.

19
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:56,680
Encoders report wheel response,
not ground speed.

20
00:00:57,200 --> 00:00:59,640
Today, radar feeds our decision pipeline.

21
00:01:00,460 --> 00:01:03,060
Other sensors supply observations
and health information.

22
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:07,360
Inside the Raspberry Pi,
we ask something before trusting a reading:

23
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:10,420
is it healthy, fresh, and worth using?

24
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:13,380
Then we follow radar targets over time,

25
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:16,100
rather than treating every
detection as a new object.

26
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,520
Uncertainty travels with that track.

27
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:20,720
If an observation gets old,

28
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:24,740
the system shouldn't quietly present
yesterday's confidence as today's evidence.

29
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:28,740
Now, seeing something isn't the same
as having enough room to respond.

30
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:32,940
Time to collision asks how quickly
a closing situation could develop.

31
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:36,440
In this demo, you'll see that
value marked unavailable.

32
00:01:37,240 --> 00:01:39,900
Stopping requirement brings
together reaction distance,

33
00:01:40,320 --> 00:01:42,060
braking distance, and margin.

34
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:46,840
Comparing that requirement with observable
distance gives us the next question:

35
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:49,720
what operation can the evidence justify?

36
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:54,280
We call that the Perception-Verified
Safe Operating Envelope.

37
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:55,680
The idea is simple:

38
00:01:56,240 --> 00:02:00,180
weaker evidence should mean less
operating freedom, not more guesswork.

39
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:05,460
NORMAL, RESTRICT, STOP,
and UNKNOWN make that judgement visible.

40
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:07,360
UNKNOWN is important.

41
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,840
It says we don't have enough
trustworthy information.

42
00:02:10,660 --> 00:02:15,100
A missing sensor or stale message must
never quietly create permission to do more.

43
00:02:15,860 --> 00:02:16,900
The Pi handles reasoning.

44
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:21,800
The ESP thirty-two software checks
bounded commands before accepting them.

45
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:24,280
Separately, our electrical design

46
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:28,020
gives the emergency stop an independent
path through the contactor system.

47
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,680
Those responsibilities stay separate.

48
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:33,240
And the dashboard never
controls the vehicle.

49
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:37,220
In this software demonstration,
traction stays disabled;

50
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:38,940
neither a browser click

51
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:42,060
nor a healthy-looking screen
grants motion authority.

52
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:46,100
Location answers another question:
where are we on the map?

53
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:51,200
That's the role of GNSS
and the intended LC twenty-nine H receiver.

54
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:54,880
But radar asks where a target
is relative to our vehicle.

55
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,440
We keep those coordinate systems separate.

56
00:02:58,040 --> 00:02:59,160
Without a vehicle fix,

57
00:02:59,500 --> 00:03:04,060
the map uses a clearly labelled India
overview, not an invented location.

58
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:06,840
And this is the actual TARK dashboard.

59
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:09,220
You're looking at our public software demo.

60
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:11,300
The simulation labels stay visible.

61
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:17,500
You can inspect system state, sensor health,
communication, maps, logs, and replay.

62
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:20,600
When a sensor is missing,
the interface says so.

63
00:03:20,940 --> 00:03:25,040
For us, the important part is being
able to ask why a decision happened,

64
00:03:25,380 --> 00:03:26,980
and trace the evidence behind it.

65
00:03:27,520 --> 00:03:28,980
We've brought sensing interfaces,

66
00:03:29,280 --> 00:03:34,320
reasoning, safety logic, and bounded
control together with a real dashboard.

67
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:38,400
One testable software system,
not a collection of parts.

68
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:41,160
The goal was never blind trust in a sensor.

69
00:03:41,820 --> 00:03:43,100
Trust the evidence you can verify.

70
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:45,180
That's TARK.
